---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Sources and licenses · JLPT Reviewer">
  <article class="grid max-w-[72ch] gap-6 py-8">
    <header class="grid gap-2">
      <span class="label-mono">About</span>
      <h1 class="font-heading text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight">Sources and licenses</h1>
      <p class="text-muted-foreground">
        This review follows the method of the Nihongo Sou Matome series: weeks of six lessons and a test, vocabulary taught by
        everyday topic, kanji met in real-world text, and grammar taught three look-alike patterns at a time. It contains no
        content from those books.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="grid gap-2">
      <h2 class="font-heading text-xl font-semibold">Data</h2>
      <ul class="grid list-disc gap-2 pl-5">
        <li>
          Word readings, meanings and example sentence links: <a class="text-primary-strong underline underline-offset-4" href="https://www.edrdg.org/wiki/index.php/JMdict-EDICT_Dictionary_Project">JMdict</a>,
          © Electronic Dictionary Research and Development Group, <a class="text-primary-strong underline underline-offset-4" href="https://www.edrdg.org/edrdg/licence.html">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>,
          in the JSON form made by <a class="text-primary-strong underline underline-offset-4" href="https://github.com/scriptin/jmdict-simplified">jmdict-simplified</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Example sentences: <a class="text-primary-strong underline underline-offset-4" href="https://tatoeba.org">Tatoeba</a>, CC BY 2.0 FR, as linked from JMdict.
        </li>
        <li>
          JLPT word levels: lists by <a class="text-primary-strong underline underline-offset-4" href="http://www.tanos.co.uk/jlpt/">Jonathan Waller</a>, CC BY, via
          <a class="text-primary-strong underline underline-offset-4" href="https://github.com/jamsinclair/open-anki-jlpt-decks">open-anki-jlpt-decks</a> (MIT). The JLPT has published no official lists since 2010, so levels are approximate.
        </li>
        <li>
          Kanji readings, meanings, stroke counts and levels: KANJIDIC, © EDRDG, CC BY-SA 4.0, via
          <a class="text-primary-strong underline underline-offset-4" href="https://github.com/davidluzgouveia/kanji-data">kanji-data</a> (MIT). Only the KANJIDIC fields are used.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
        The generated data files on this site are derived from CC BY-SA sources and are shared under CC BY-SA 4.0. The site's code is MIT licensed.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="grid gap-2">
      <h2 class="font-heading text-xl font-semibold">Written for this review</h2>
      <p>
        Grammar lessons, their examples and test items, and the topic grouping of words were written for this review. They can contain mistakes.
        Every item has a flag button. Flagged items leave your cards and drills until you clear the flag.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="grid gap-2">
      <h2 class="font-heading text-xl font-semibold">How the course is built</h2>
      <ul class="grid list-disc gap-2 pl-5">
        <li>Weeks 1–2: an N4 placement sort, then twelve N4 grammar refresh lessons.</li>
        <li>Weeks 3–20: six themes of three weeks each. Each lesson rechecks N4 words on its topic, teaches about 14 N3 words, introduces the kanji those words use, and ends with an 8-item practice.</li>
        <li>Weeks 3–12 add three grammar patterns per lesson. Day 7 of every week is a timed test in JLPT question formats.</li>
        <li>Cards are scheduled with FSRS, at a desired retention of 0.90 by default.</li>
        <li>Audio uses your browser's Japanese voice. Quality depends on your system.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
